## TL;DR  

*The current test – *“`text‑anchor` is **undefined** **and** `symbol‑placement` is the literal string **`'point'`**”* – is **not a reliable way to recognise a route‑shield layer**.  
*Place‑name (or any other point‑symbol) layers often omit `text‑anchor` (the default is *center*) and they may also be set to `symbol‑placement: 'point'`. That makes the rule fire on *every* point‑symbol that has a text field, and it will miss shields that use a zoom‑function or an expression for `symbol‑placement` (the OSM Bright/Liberty shields do exactly that).

Below is a short “why it fails” and a **practical, more‑robust heuristic** you can adopt in your audit script.

---

## 1. Why the current heuristic is unsound  

| Property | What the spec says | What many “plain” layers do |
|----------|-------------------|-----------------------------|
| `text-anchor` | Optional – defaults to **`'center'`** if omitted. | Most place‑name layers **don’t set it** because the default is fine. |
| `symbol-placement` | Can be a **string** (`'point'`, `'line'`, `'line-center'`) **or an expression** (stops, case, match, etc.). | Many point‑symbol layers set it to the literal `'point'`. Shield layers in the OSM Bright/Liberty styles use a **zoom‑function** (`{ "base": 1, "stops": [[7,"point"],[8,"line"]] }`) – not the plain string. |
| `icon-image` | For shields it is usually an **expression** that interpolates `{network}` and `{ref_length}` (e.g. `"{network}_{ref_length}"`). | Place‑name layers either have **no `icon-image`** or a **static name** (`'city'`, `'town'`, …). |
| `text-field` | Shields always have a **text‑field** that pulls the road reference (`'{ref}'`). | Place‑name layers also have a `text-field` (often `'{name}'`). |
| `icon-text-fit` | Shields that use *stretchable* badges set this to `"both"`/`"width"`/`"height"`; the OSM Bright/Liberty “pre‑drawn” shields leave it at the default (`'none'`). | Most POI layers leave it at the default as well. |

Because **`text-anchor` is frequently omitted** and **`symbol-placement` can be a literal `'point'`** for many non‑shield layers, the rule you have now will:

* **Flag every point‑symbol that has a text field** (city, park, mountain, etc.) as a shield.  
* **Miss real shield layers** whose `symbol-placement` is expressed as a zoom‑function or that belong to the `default` sheet (they still have `symbol-placement` but not the plain string).

---

## 2. What a shield *actually* looks like in the style spec  

Below is a distilled version of the OSM Bright shield layer (the same pattern appears in OSM Liberty, OpenMapTiles, etc.):

```json
{
  "id": "highway-shield-us-interstate",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "transportation_name",
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "{network}_{ref_length}",          // ← expression with placeholders
    "text-field": "{ref}",                           // ← road number
    "symbol-placement": {
      "base": 1,
      "stops": [
        [7, "point"],
        [8, "line"]
      ]
    },
    "text-font": ["Open Sans Bold", "Arial Unicode MS Bold"],
    "text-size": 12,
    "text-allow-overlap": true,
    "icon-allow-overlap": true,
    // optional – stretchable shields would also have:
    // "icon-text-fit": "both",
    // "icon-text-fit-padding": [2, 2, 2, 2],
    // "icon-size": 1
  }
}
```

Key things that **distinguish a shield** from a generic place‑name layer:

| Feature | Shield‑specific value | Typical place‑name value |
|---------|----------------------|--------------------------|
| **`icon-image`** | **Expression** containing `{network}` and/or `{ref_length}` (or a similar placeholder pattern). | Either **absent** or a **static** name (`'city'`, `'park'`, …). |
| **`text-field`** | **Expression** that resolves to the road reference (`'{ref}'` or `'{ref}'`‑like). | Usually `'{name}'` or another property, not `ref`. |
| **`symbol-placement`** | **Expression** (zoom‑function) *or* the literal `'point'` **only when the layer is a point‑shield** (e.g. for isolated interstates). | Usually a **plain string** `'point'` (or omitted, defaulting to `'point'`). |
| **`icon-text-fit`** (optional) | `"both"` / `"width"` / `"height"` for *stretchable* shields. | Usually omitted (`'none'`). |
| **`icon-allow-overlap` / `text-allow-overlap`** | Often set to `true` (shields must stay visible). | May be `false` (default) for POI labels. |
| **`icon-size`** | May be a **function** that scales with zoom (e.g. `["interpolate", ["linear"], ["zoom"], 5, 0.5, 10, 1]`). | Usually a constant or omitted. |

The **only two properties that *must* be present** for a shield are:

1. **`icon-image`** – an expression that references a *placeholder* (`{network}`, `{ref_length}`, `{ref}` etc.).
2. **`text-field`** – an expression that references the road reference (`{ref}`).

Everything else is optional or style‑dependent.

---

## 3. A more reliable heuristic  

Below is a **self‑contained JavaScript function** you can drop into your audit script. It returns `true` only when a layer looks like a route‑shield layer.

```js
/**
 * Returns true if a symbol layer appears to be a route‑shield layer.
 *
 * @param {Object} layer – a layer object from a MapLibre style.
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isRouteShieldLayer(layer) {
  // 1️⃣ Must be a symbol layer.
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout || {};

  // 2️⃣ Must have a text field that references a road reference.
  //    Typical placeholder: {ref} (could also be {ref:short} etc.).
  const textField = layout['text-field'];
  if (typeof textField !== 'string' || !/\{ref\}/.test(textField)) return false;

  // 3️⃣ Must have an icon‑image that is *dynamic* (contains a placeholder).
  //    Shields use {network} and/or {ref_length}.  A static name like "cafe"
  //    will not contain braces.
  const iconImage = layout['icon-image'];
  if (typeof iconImage !== 'string' || !/\{(network|ref[_-]length)\}/.test(iconImage))
    return false;

  // 4️⃣ (Optional but helpful) – symbol‑placement is either a function
  //    or the literal "point".  If it is a function we accept it; if it is
  //    a plain string we still accept "point" because many shields are
  //    point‑only at low zoom.
  const placement = layout['symbol-placement'];
  const isPlacementOk =
    typeof placement === 'string' ? placement === 'point' : true; // function is fine
  if (!isPlacementOk) return false;

  // 5️⃣ (Optional) – shields usually allow overlap so they stay visible.
  //    This is not a hard rule, but it helps filter out POI layers that
  //    happen to have a placeholder in the icon name.
  const allowOverlap = layout['icon-allow-overlap'] ?? false;
  if (!allowOverlap) {
    // If the layer explicitly disables overlap, it is unlikely a shield.
    // (Some custom styles may turn it off, so we only *reject* when the
    //  property exists and is false.)
    if ('icon-allow-overlap' in layout) return false;
  }

  // 6️⃣ (Optional) – stretchable shields have icon-text-fit !== 'none'.
  //    If present, it should be one of the allowed values.
  if ('icon-text-fit' in layout) {
    const fit = layout['icon-text-fit'];
    if (!['both', 'width', 'height'].includes(fit)) return false;
  }

  // All checks passed → looks like a shield.
  return true;
}
```

### Why this works

| Check | What it catches | What it discards |
|-------|----------------|------------------|
| **`text-field` contains `{ref}`** | Guarantees the layer is trying to render a road number. | Excludes place‑name layers that use `{name}` or a static string. |
| **`icon-image` contains `{network}` or `{ref_length}`** | Shields need a *badge* that varies by network and length. | Excludes static icons (`'cafe'`, `'city'`, …). |
| **`symbol-placement` is a function *or* `'point'`** | Accepts both the OSM Bright/Liberty zoom‑function and simple point‑only shields. | Prevents false‑positives from layers that deliberately use `'line'` or `'line-center'`. |
| **`icon-allow-overlap`** (optional) | Shields normally set this to `true` so the badge never disappears. | Filters out many POI layers that keep the default `false`. |
| **`icon-text-fit`** (optional) | Recognises the *stretchable* badge pattern (`both`, `width`, `height`). | Still works for the pre‑drawn badge style where the property is omitted. |

You can **tune the optional parts** (steps 5‑6) to match the exact style you audit. If you only need to detect *any* shield (pre‑drawn or stretchable) the first three checks are sufficient and will correctly ignore ordinary place‑name layers.

---

## 4. Quick sanity‑check against common false‑positives  

| Layer type | `text-field` | `icon-image` | Result |
|------------|--------------|--------------|--------|
| **City label** | `"{name}"` | *none* or `"city"` | **false** (fails #2) |
| **Park POI** | `"{name}"` | `"park"` | **false** (fails #2 & #3) |
| **Custom point‑icon with dynamic name** | `"{name}"` | `"{category}"` | **false** (fails #2) |
| **OSM Bright interstate shield** | `"{ref}"` | `"{network}_{ref_length}"` | **true** |
| **OSM Liberty state‑highway shield (stretchable)** | `"{ref}"` | `"{network}_{ref_length}"` | **true** (passes #3, optional #6 catches stretchable) |
| **A line‑shield that switches to point at low zoom** | `"{ref}"` | `"{network}_{ref_length}"` | **true** (placement is a function, accepted) |

---

## 5. How to integrate it into your existing audit script  

If your script currently does something like:

```js
if (layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined &&
    layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point') {
  // tag as shield
}
```

Replace it with:

```js
if (isRouteShieldLayer(layer)) {
  // tag as shield
}
```

You can keep the old check as a *fallback* for very old styles that pre‑date the placeholder convention, but **guard it with a warning** that it is a heuristic only for legacy data.

